malcolm.ryan@gmail.com wrote:
> This whole (half / full) magic resistance vs (half / full) spell
> resistance thing seems to me to contain so many arbitrary distinctions
> that it would only make any sense to a seriously spoiled player. On
> that basis, I don't think it is worth the trouble.

Everyone who wins at Nethack is seriously spoiled, for some reasonable
value of "seriously."  An unspoiled Nethack player is a dead one --
this is true to the degree that, in order for the game to be (barely)
fair to an unenlightened player, the game contains its own spoilers in
the form of the Oracle's major consultations.

To give an example, and to get back to the subject of resistances: my
first deep dungeon game ended in the Castle.  After reading a bit more,
my next game ended in the Valley of the Dead, when I discovered you
shouldn't pray there.  My next good game ended in melee combat with the
Wizard, me without magic resistance.  That was a particularly bad death
for me, because I hadn't yet discovered the group, and I hadn't read
the spoiler covering magic resistance.  I almost dropped the game as
"too hard" at that point.  Some time after that point, I sat down with
the spoilers available at that time and read through them throughly.
Thus, I was prepared for the Wizard next time and Astral too.

My point is, practically every player who wins these days is spoiled to
some degree.  Thus, it is nonsensical to talk about not including some
feature because it only makes sense to spoiled players, when so many of
them will be spoiled as a consequence of needing to be to be good at
the game.

